---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Gasóleo vs Gasolina: Fuelling a Rental Car in Portugal | TripAxle"
  description="Gasóleo is diesel and gasolina is petrol. How to tell which your rental car takes, what the pump colours mean, fuel policies, and what to do if you misfuel."
  eyebrow="Practical Guides"
  summary="Gasóleo means diesel; gasolina means petrol. A quick guide to reading the pumps, confirming what your car takes, and handling the fuel policy on your rental."
  updated="2026-03-15"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  related={related}
>
  <QuickAnswer slot="answer">
    <p>
      <strong>Gasóleo = diesel. Gasolina = petrol (gasoline).</strong> Most
      Portuguese rental cars are diesel, but never assume — check the fuel flap,
      the key tag, or ask at the desk, and confirm before the nozzle goes in.
      Diesel pumps are usually black; petrol pumps green (95) or often a darker
      shade for 98.
    </p>
  </QuickAnswer>

  <h2 id="words">The two words that matter</h2>
  <p>
    Portuguese fuel stations label pumps in Portuguese. The only two words you
    strictly need:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gasóleo</strong> — diesel. Sometimes shown as “Gasóleo simples,” “Gasóleo especial,” or a branded premium diesel.</li>
    <li><strong>Gasolina</strong> — petrol. Usually “Gasolina 95” and “Gasolina 98,” with 95 the standard grade.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The trap for English speakers is that “gasolina” looks and sounds like
    “gasoline,” so a diesel car can be misfuelled by reflex. If your car is
    diesel, the word you want is the one that looks less familiar: gasóleo.
  </p>

  <h2 id="which">How to know which your car takes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Look inside the fuel filler flap — many cars have a “Diesel only” / “Gasóleo” sticker there.</li>
    <li>Check the key fob tag or the rental paperwork; some companies write the fuel type on it.</li>
    <li>Ask at the desk when you collect the car and note the answer.</li>
    <li>A diesel filler neck is wider; a modern petrol car often has a smaller opening with a green ring.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout variant="tip">
    Take a photo of the open fuel flap when you pick the car up. If there is a
    fuel-type label there, you now have it on your phone for every fill-up.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="station">At the fuel station</h2>
  <p>
    Most stations on motorways and in towns are self-service (“self” or
    “automática”). Pull up, note the pump number, and either pay at the pump by
    card or pay the cashier inside afterwards by quoting the pump number.
    Attended stations exist in rural areas and simply add a small service
    premium.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Diesel nozzles/handles are commonly black; petrol 95 is commonly green.</li>
    <li>Colours are a hint, not a guarantee — always read the word on the pump.</li>
    <li>“Aditivado” or premium grades are fine to use but cost more; the standard grade is all a rental car needs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="policy">Fuel policies and returning the car</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Full-to-full</strong> (most common and usually best value): you get a full tank and return it full. Keep the receipt from a station near the drop-off.</li>
    <li><strong>Full-to-empty / pre-purchase</strong>: you pay for a tank up front and return it as empty as you dare, with no refund for what is left. Often poor value unless you will genuinely run it down.</li>
    <li>Some off-airport suppliers charge a refuelling or “service” fee on top — check the terms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a full-to-full rental, fill up within a few kilometres of the return
    location and keep the timestamped receipt in case of a dispute.
  </p>

  <h2 id="misfuel">If you put in the wrong fuel</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Do not start the engine.</strong> Starting it circulates the wrong
    fuel through the system and turns a drain-and-flush into a major repair.
    Leave the car where it is, call the rental company's assistance number on
    your paperwork, and tell them what happened. Misfuelling is usually treated
    as driver responsibility, so the cost is likely yours, but it is far smaller
    if the engine never ran.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
